<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      The 10-day app blockade should survive the app being uninstalled and reinstalled. App-private
      storage is wiped on uninstall, so uninstall-survival is best-effort via Android Auto Backup:
      the internal ledger (blockade.json) is included in cloud backup (see @xml/backup_rules and
      @xml/data_extraction_rules) and restored on reinstall, keyed to the user's Google account. This
      requires no runtime permission.

      The 鍛錬 training history (exercise.db) is included in the same two rule files, main database
      file only. A year of training is worth restoring, and there is nothing sensitive in it.

      鍛錬 adds one component: the health foreground service declared at the foot of this file
      (00-plan.md §6 Phase 3). It is NOT what makes a backgrounded session correct — the player keeps
      the screen on with FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON and derives its timeline from a monotonic clock, which
      03-player.md §E.5 documents as already correct on return without any service. What the service
      buys is the audio and haptic cue channel surviving a pocket, and it is written so that a user who
      grants nothing trains exactly as they did in Phase 1.
    -->

    <!--
      The session player's haptic channel: a pulse at each phase change, so a countdown can be felt
      through a pocket rather than watched. VIBRATE is a normal install-time permission — it is granted
      at install, there is no runtime prompt and no user-visible ceremony, which is why it does not
      engage 03-player.md §E.5's Phase-1 refusal of runtime and foreground-service entries. Vibrator
      calls are unconditional in GymHaptics, so without this the whole channel is silently inert and
      lint fails with MissingPermission.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <!--
      The 予定 page reads the device's calendar through CalendarContract — the same provider the
      Google Calendar sync adapter writes into, so Tempo shows the very agenda the user's other
      devices show, with no OAuth, no network and no Play Services. WRITE lets the composer add and
      edit an event in place; the change is picked up by the sync adapter and pushed back to Google.
      Both are requested at the moment the user opens the page, never at launch.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CALENDAR" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WRITE_CALENDAR" />

    <!--
      GYM.SESSION.COMPLETE's 予定に入れる hand-off adds NO permission: it writes through the very
      CalendarRepository the 予定 composer writes through, behind the same EventConfirmDialog gate, so
      the two lines above are the whole of what it needs (04-library-records.md §4 row 8).
    -->

    <!--
      The health foreground service (gym/TrainingService.kt).

      FOREGROUND_SERVICE and FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH are normal install-time permissions: they are
      the right to run the service at all, and neither prompts.

      POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a RUNTIME permission from API 33. It is requested exactly once, on the
      record screen after a session has ended (TrainingConsentMount), and never on launch and never in
      front of a workout. A denial suppresses the notification and the service still runs.

      ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION is a RUNTIME permission and is here for one concrete, checkable reason: from
      API 34 a service declaring foregroundServiceType="health" may only call startForeground() while
      the app holds at least one of BODY_SENSORS, READ_HEART_RATE, READ_SKIN_TEMPERATURE,
      READ_OXYGEN_SATURATION or ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION. Without one of them the service cannot be created
      at all. It is therefore requested only on API 34+ (TrainingConsent.required), and a denial costs
      the notification, never the workout — startForeground is wrapped and the service stops itself.

      REJECTED: HIGH_SAMPLING_RATE_SENSORS, which satisfies the same API 34 prerequisite by manifest
      declaration alone with no runtime prompt. Tempo reads no sensor at any rate, and taking a sensor
      permission by install-time default *because* the user cannot decline it is worse than asking for
      the one they can.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION" />

    <!--
      A home app legitimately needs to see every launchable app to populate Search.
      The launcher intent below grants that visibility on Android 11+ without QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES.
      The second entry lets Tempo hand a repeating event off to the system calendar app, which is the
      only place recurrence is safe to edit.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:mimeType="vnd.android.cursor.item/event" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Tempo">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:stateNotNeeded="true"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:configChanges="keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|density|uiMode"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- Surfaces real notifications to the 通知 screen once the user grants access. -->
        <service
            android:name=".notification.TempoNotificationListener"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          鍛錬's live session, held in the foreground so its cues survive a pocket (00-plan.md §6).

          exported="false": the two notification actions are PendingIntents this app creates for
          itself, and nothing outside Tempo has any business starting a workout.

          stopWithTask="true": one of three independent guarantees that the service never outlives a
          session. The other two are in Kotlin — START_NOT_STICKY and the mount's onDispose — because
          a stuck foreground notification is worse than no notification at all.

          It is three and not four because this attribute *removes* a hook rather than adding one:
          with FLAG_STOP_WITH_TASK set the framework stops the service itself and Service.onTaskRemoved
          is never delivered, so an override for it is dead code claiming a guarantee. Setting this and
          overriding that is the contradiction TrainingManifestTest refuses.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".gym.TrainingService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="health"
            android:stopWithTask="true" />

    </application>

</manifest>
